\section{Find a Dead Result Without Erasing a Live Effect}

Compile the toy function below using an instruction-level control-flow graph (CFG). The parameters are an integer \(k\) and a boolean flag. Addition wraps, while division can trap on zero. The locals have no aliases and cannot be inspected from outside the function; normal returns and traps are observable. There are no exception handlers or callbacks. Our goal is to remove assignments whose values are dead without changing those observations.

\begin{verbatim}
10: x := 7
20: y := x + 1
30: t := 2
40: q := 4 / k
50: if flag goto 60 else goto 70
60: return 0
70: return 1
\end{verbatim}

The extractor supplies normal CFG edges, the variables an instruction reads and writes, and an independent \texttt{discardable} certificate. Here it issues the certificate only for assignments with a single fall-through successor, guaranteed to finish normally and to have no observable effects other than writing their one local result. In particular, it never certifies instruction \(40\). This is the same kind of extractor/consumer boundary that makes a declarative pass replaceable: change the extractor when target semantics change, and validate the assumptions made by the consumer. Khedker, Sanyal, and Karkare describe liveness as a backwards dataflow analysis; the certificate is an additional condition for removal, not part of liveness.

\section{Compute Liveness Before Testing Absence}

The executable Souffl\'e encoding uses number IDs for instruction sites and symbols for variables. The final negation reads the settled \texttt{live\_out} relation. Negation of \texttt{def} is also safe: it reads a fixed input relation, and the pair tested is bound by \texttt{live\_out}.

\begin{verbatim}
.decl edge(s:number, t:number)
.decl use_at(s:number, v:symbol)
.decl def(s:number, v:symbol)
.decl discardable(s:number)
.decl live_in(s:number, v:symbol)
.decl live_out(s:number, v:symbol)
.decl removable(s:number, v:symbol)
.output live_out
.output removable

edge(10,20). edge(20,30). edge(30,40). edge(40,50).
edge(50,60). edge(50,70).
def(10,"x"). def(20,"y"). def(30,"t"). def(40,"q").
use_at(20,"x"). use_at(40,"k"). use_at(50,"flag").
discardable(10). discardable(20). discardable(30).

live_in(S,V) :- use_at(S,V).
live_out(S,V) :- edge(S,T), live_in(T,V).
live_in(S,V) :- live_out(S,V), !def(S,V).
removable(S,V) :- discardable(S), def(S,V), !live_out(S,V).
\end{verbatim}

In the baseline, the \texttt{live\_out} facts, grouped by instruction, are:

\begin{verbatim}
10: {"flag","k","x"}
20: {"flag","k"}
30: {"flag","k"}
40: {"flag"}
50,60,70: {}
removable(20,"y"). removable(30,"t").
\end{verbatim}

Instruction \(20\) uses \(x\) even if \(y\) is subsequently unused. Instruction \(40\) reads \(k\), and has no live result \(q\), yet can still trap. Removing it would turn the call with \(k=0\) from a trap into a return. If the language supported local handlers, their CFG edges and use summaries would also need to be modeled. The fixed-input transfer \(U\cup(S\setminus D)\) is monotone in \(S\); the final absence test is not monotone in added live-out facts. It is therefore unsound to issue removal decisions from a partially saturated liveness relation.

\Cref{fig:visual-351} marks which original CFG nodes the pass can remove, which it must retain, and where an edit can create a new use.

