% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Apply One Certified Rewrite, Then Repeat}

\begin{verbatim}
repeat:
    facts := extract(current_ir)          # all rows from one snapshot
    choices := run_to_completion(facts).removable
    if choices is empty: stop
    site := first_in_source_order(choices)
    bypass(site)                         # reconnect predecessors
    validate_cfg_and_entry()             # update entry if needed
\end{verbatim}

The first rewrite bypasses \(20\), so its use of \(x\) is no longer in the input. The next analysis emits \verb|removable(10,"x")| and \verb|removable(30,"t")|; eventually only \(40,50,60,70\) remain. Each successful rewrite removes an instruction, so the outer loop terminates for this finite IR. This loop is not the inner fixed point: a rewrite deletes facts and can change both the starting point and the transfer functions. For a direct end-to-end test, run the initial and final IR with both flag inputs at \(k=2\) (returns \(0\) or \(1\)) and at \(k=0\) (traps for both).

As a second input, change instruction \(70\) to \texttt{return y}, adding \verb|use_at(70,"y").| to the original facts. Now \verb|live_out(20,"y")| holds because at least one successor path uses the value, and only \verb|removable(30,"t")| remains. With \(k=2\) and a false flag the result is \(8\), not \(1\). Compare the rule output with a simple backwards worklist on both snapshots. PQL demonstrates a different user of program facts: its authors translate a static approximation into Datalog for bddbddb and use analysis results to reduce runtime instrumentation. Our case study uses neither its query language nor its engine; the shared idea is to keep the evidence separate from the compiler action it permits.

The optimizer's actionable output is therefore \texttt{removable} together with its extractor certificate and IR version, not liveness alone. Revalidate those inputs after each edit before emitting the rewritten function.
